---
import DocumentLayout from '../layouts/DocumentLayout.astro';
import LeadStrand from '../components/LeadStrand.astro';
import Receipt from '../components/Receipt.astro';
import Needs from '../components/Needs.astro';
import Draft from '../components/Draft.astro';
---

<DocumentLayout
  title="I replaced an agency handoff process with a pipeline that returns working code"
  metaTitle="An AI-native design-to-code pipeline | Tyler Young"
  description="Replacing an agency handoff process with a Figma-to-Claude-Code pipeline producing production React, Tailwind and Storybook components."
  descriptionDraft
  eyebrow="01 · AI-Native Practice"
  meta={[
    ['Role', 'Director of Design · 90-day engagement · 2026'],
    ['Built', 'Design system v0 · Figma↔Claude Code pipeline'],
    ['Stack', 'React · Tailwind · Storybook'],
    ['Outcome', '[NEEDS: Tyler’s one-liner]'],
  ]}
>
  <p class="answer">
    <Draft>
      An agency sat between design intent and shipped UI, and every decision made a
      round trip through a handoff document. I replaced the document with a pipeline:
      Figma to Claude Code to React, Tailwind and Storybook, with the design system
      as the constraint on what could be generated. Stakeholder reviews moved onto
      running components.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <Receipt
    label="~/design-system — prompt to rendered component"
    leftLabel="Instruction"
    rightLabel="Rendered in Storybook"
    caption="The loop that replaced the handoff document."
  >
    <div slot="left">
      <Needs block ratio="4 / 3">
        3–4 stills or a 60–90s capture: prompt in Claude Code → generated component
        → rendered result
      </Needs>
    </div>
    <div slot="right">
      <Needs block ratio="4 / 3">Storybook screenshot — component grid / library view</Needs>
    </div>
  </Receipt>

  <h2>How it worked</h2>

  <h3>The system is the constraint, not the deliverable</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      Design system v0 came out of real surfaces — the landing page, the context
      engine, the workflow designer — not from a component wishlist. That matters
      because the system is what bounds generation. A wishlist produces components
      nobody asked for; real surfaces produce the ones that had to exist.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>Reviews happen on the thing, not a picture of the thing</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      A mockup review postpones the argument about whether something works. A review
      on a rendered component ends it. This was the change stakeholders noticed
      first, and it cost nothing to adopt.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <h3>Exploration is cheap, so judgment is the constraint</h3>
  <p>
    <Draft>
      When options come back as working code in minutes, the scarce thing stops being
      production capacity and becomes knowing which option is right and what
      guardrails it has to respect on the way out.
    </Draft>
  </p>

  <div class="aside">
    <p>
      Open decision — naming. This page avoids naming the client. Name it, or
      describe it as “an AR / asset-management company”? Tyler's call. Also held:
      whether the team-assessment thread is public or interview-only.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2>What changed</h2>
  <p>
    <Needs>
      3–5 real, verifiable statements. Honest scale: 90 days, v0, process proved.
      "Stakeholders reviewed working code for the first time" is strong enough
      without embellishment.
    </Needs>
  </p>

  <LeadStrand
    slot="after"
    items={[
      {
        slug: 'how-i-scaled-design-efficiency',
        note: 'The pipeline is the same argument as the design system that came before it: teams move faster when the shared thing is assumed rather than optional. That one was adopted through usefulness, not mandate — this one had to be too.',
      },
      {
        slug: 'how-i-shaped-cross-functional-reputation',
        note: 'Moving reviews onto working code changes who design is to engineering and product. It is a reputational move as much as a technical one, and it only holds if the design org has already earned a seat in planning.',
      },
    ]}
  />
</DocumentLayout>

<style>
  .answer {
    font-size: 24px !important;
    line-height: 1.52 !important;
    margin-bottom: 40px !important;
  }
</style>
